OBD Intelligence
Complete Android product brief, UX blueprint, technical architecture, implementation plan and acceptance criteria
 
Prepared as a hand-off specification for Claude Code / Claude. The intent is that the developer can use this document as the single product brief, inspect the referenced sources, create the project, implement it in stages, and deliver a working Android application.
Vehicle baseline (verify before vehicle-specific work)
Target vehicle context supplied by the owner: Kia Sportage, GCC-market, 2.0 MPI petrol AWD, approximately model year 2019. The owner has used an ELM327 adapter and Car Scanner ELM OBD2. Exact model year, ECU identifiers, adapter chipset/firmware and Bluetooth transport must be confirmed in the app onboarding and by a real scan. Do not hard-code unsupported assumptions.

Document version: 1.0  |  Date: 1 November 2026  |  Target: Android 9 (API 28) and newer
 
0. Instructions to Claude — read this first
Primary instruction
Treat this document as the product requirements document (PRD), design brief and engineering hand-off. Build a real, installable Android Studio project—not just a mock-up. Work in milestones, compile and test after each milestone, and do not claim hardware functions work until tested against an ELM327 adapter and vehicle or clearly labelled as simulated.

•	Start by inspecting the available repository and development environment. If no project exists, create a new Kotlin Android project. If a repository is supplied later, inspect it before changing files.
•	Before coding, produce a short implementation plan, identify any conflicting assumptions, and list external credentials needed (for example, Google Maps API key). Do not stop for non-blocking questions; use the safe defaults in this document.
•	Build the standard OBD2 engine and responsive user interface independently. Keep hardware access behind interfaces so the UI can run in Demo Mode without a connected vehicle.
•	Use real protocol specifications and verified vehicle/ECU data. Never invent a Kia PID, ECU address, DTC definition, sensor value, or successful test result.
•	All displayed readings must show units, source, freshness, and whether they are live, calculated, estimated, unavailable, or demo data.
•	Create a README with setup, build, run, permissions, adapter pairing, troubleshooting, test procedure, and known limitations.
•	Deliver the complete source project, Gradle wrapper, build instructions, debug APK if the environment permits, and a test report. Do not silently omit incomplete modules.
Definition of done
•	Project builds successfully with the documented Gradle/JDK/Android Gradle Plugin versions.
•	Installs and runs on Android 9+ and adapts to Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra portrait and landscape layouts.
•	Demo Mode makes every screen navigable and visually complete without a vehicle.
•	ELM327 connection, adapter identification, PID discovery, live data, DTC read/clear confirmation and logging work with supported adapters.
•	Map/Waze hand-off is implemented honestly: Waze is launched externally via supported deep links; no unsupported embedded Waze map is claimed.
•	Horizontal dashboard pages, page customisation, settings persistence, responsive layouts and safe driving restrictions work.
•	Automated tests cover protocol parsing, PID calculations, DTC parsing, data freshness, persistence and UI state.
Product vision
Create a premium, landscape-first automotive cockpit and OBD2 diagnostic companion for the owner's Kia Sportage, while remaining useful on a Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra in portrait and landscape. The app combines a live vehicle dashboard, navigation hand-off, multiple horizontally swipeable dashboards, diagnostics, fuel analytics, trips, maintenance and exportable health reports.
The application is OBD2-first and vehicle-aware—not a claim to replace Kia KDS/GDS dealership diagnostics. Support for manufacturer-specific modules depends on the actual vehicle ECU, adapter, protocol, permissions and verified reverse-engineered information.
Product principles
•	Responsive rather than fixed-resolution: adapt to available width, height, orientation, density and window size.
•	Truthful telemetry: never fabricate values or display stale values as live.
•	Offline-first: core OBD, dashboard, logs and vehicle history work locally without cloud accounts.
•	Read-only by default: no arbitrary CAN writes, ECU flashing, immobilizer operations or safety-system actuation.
•	Safe while moving: large glanceable controls; detailed diagnostics and configuration require parked state.
•	Modular: generic SAE OBD engine underneath, vehicle profiles and optional Kia-enhanced capability above.
 
