4. OBD protocol and ELM327 engineering
4.1 Standard OBD service modes
Mode	Meaning	Implementation notes
01	Current powertrain data	PID discovery, typed decoders, units and support checks.
02	Freeze-frame data	Frame index and associated DTC; not every ECU supports all PIDs.
03	Stored DTCs	Parse response bytes; handle multiple codes and no-code response.
04	Clear/reset emissions DTC information	Explicit confirmation; warn that readiness and freeze-frame data may be reset.
05	Oxygen sensor monitoring test results	Legacy/vehicle-dependent; use only when ECU supports it.
06	On-board monitoring test results	Decode only known test IDs/limits; preserve raw data.
07	Pending DTCs	Separate from stored and permanent codes.
08	Control of onboard systems	Do not expose generic control in consumer UI; restricted and not part of initial release.
09	Vehicle information	VIN, calibration IDs, CVNs where supported; multi-frame handling.
0A	Permanent DTCs	Read-only; permanent DTCs are not cleared by Mode 04.

4.2 ELM327 command lifecycle
1.	Open transport (Bluetooth SPP RFCOMM or BLE GATT).
2.	Initialize adapter with documented AT commands: reset, echo off, linefeeds off, spaces on/off consistently, headers configuration as required, protocol auto or selected.
3.	Read adapter identification; detect timeout, malformed responses and likely clone limitations.
4.	Send one command at a time through a central serialized command queue. Never run independent polling loops against the same socket.
5.	Normalize response lines, strip prompts/echo, handle NO DATA, STOPPED, BUS INIT errors, question marks and buffer overflow.
6.	Parse positive/negative responses, ECU headers, CAN multi-frame/ISO-TP responses and flow control as required.
7.	Schedule PIDs by priority and rate. Avoid flooding low-cost adapters; make polling intervals configurable and measured.
8.	On disconnect, stop queue, mark readings stale, close resources and reconnect with bounded backoff.
4.3 Architecture interfaces
interface ObdTransport {
    val connectionState: StateFlow<ConnectionState>
    suspend fun connect(device: AdapterDevice)
    suspend fun write(bytes: ByteArray)
    fun incomingBytes(): Flow<ByteArray>
    suspend fun disconnect()
}

interface ObdCommandChannel {
    suspend fun execute(command: String, timeoutMs: Long): ObdResponse
}

interface VehicleProfile {
    val id: String
    val make: String
    val model: String
    val yearRange: IntRange?
    val market: String?
    val engineCode: String?
}

interface PidDecoder<T> {
    val service: Int
    val pid: Int
    fun decode(payload: ByteArray): T
}

Use these as conceptual contracts; adapt signatures to the chosen codebase. Keep Android Bluetooth APIs in the data/transport layer, not in Compose UI or domain logic.
4.4 Standard PID examples
PID	Signal	Typical decoder (verify exact response length)
010C	Engine RPM	((A*256)+B)/4 rpm
010D	Vehicle speed	A km/h
0105	Coolant temperature	A - 40 °C
010F	Intake air temperature	A - 40 °C
0104	Calculated engine load	A*100/255 %
0111	Throttle position	A*100/255 %
0106	STFT Bank 1	(A-128)*100/128 %
